ng race the green party  has a new leader  anime paul a lawyer has made history by  becoming the first  elected black leader of a major federal  party in canada  she replaces elizabeth may who was the  face of the party  for 14 years and joining me now is the  new leader of the green party anime paul  first of all congratulations but as you  know  there is so much to do your party  managed to get three seats in the house  of commons in the last election and the  pundits  always said the greens were the ones to  watch and yet your party hasn't been  able to get the traction that is  necessary  to hold some kind of balance of power  what will you do differently from  elizabeth may  well first let's keep in mind that this  isn't  an elizabeth may issue this is really  something that is structural in our  political system  uh the way that things are designed we  give people many more reasons to vote  against  a party than for a party and we know  that that's what happens in the last  election  where over a third of people voted uh  strategically  i think that uh this pandemic and all of  the  the critical and urgent situations  associated with it  that would have been better served by  our policies  has given us a new case that we can make  to people in canada  a big part of your party is about  climate change the ndp and the liberals  have had their own green plans but  all of that is overshadowed by the  pandemic so how can you bring the center  piece of your party's existence  back to the forefront the center piece  of our party's existence in fact  is our people and the needs of people  in canada we need emergency we need  urgent benefits  that we need to create structural  changes so that this  situation never happens again and that  we're more resilient  and we've been talking about those  things forever we have been leading on  social policy  and this moment in time has really  demonstrated how incredibly important  our social policies are  you're running in toronto center's  by-election that's bill morneau's old  riding how confident are you that you  can win that seat and if you don't win  the by-election  how will you manage to lead outside of  parliament  well i did made the decision to run in  this by-election  well before the vote on the next leader  of the party  because my roots are very deep in the  writing it's a writing that i was born  in and my mother taught in  my grandmother worked as a frontline  worker in the hospitals inn  and it's a writing that has been  profoundly neglected  by candidate after candidate parachuted  in  by the liberals and so i ran because i  believe that people in toronto center  deserve real representation  i ran because i believe that enough is  enough and so  i hope that toronto center residents  will agree with me  and they have every reason to  particularly in the midst of this  pandemic  and i have become a pro on how to  campaign in in a pandemic  and i'm looking forward to applying all  of those skills  to uh to make our case to canadians over  the coming months  anime paul thank you for your time and  once again congratulations  thank you so very much take care up next  the young royals and their wild  questions for sir david  there's canadian pride at the london  marathon brent lakitas has won the men's  elite wheelchair race  the quebec native raced with his own  specially designed wheelchair crossing  the finish line in one hour  46 minutes and four seconds this is his  first major marathon victory  lakitas has seven paralympic medals  under his belt  as well as 11 world track titles  the children of the duke and duchess of  cambridge got the chance to quiz  david attenborough about nature prince  george princess starlet  and prince louis brought their wildlife  curiosities straight to the acclaimed  naturalist and broadcaster  it's rare to hear the young royal speak  and george cut to the chase  hello david attenborough what animal do  you think will become extinct  next well let's hope there won't be any  because there are lots of things we can  do when animals are reduced to  and danger of extinction we can protect  them  hello you gave your national girl i like  spiders do you like spiders too  i love spiders i'm so glad you like them  i think they're wonderful things why is  it the people so  frightened of them i think it's because  they've actually got eight legs  the virtual chat is part of a campaign  to promote attenborough's  new documentary adorable and that is  global national for this sunday i'm  robin gill  tonight your canada is the brant street  pier in burlington ontario we would love  to see your corner of this country so  please  send your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching donna friesen is  back tomorrow good night  [Music]  you
 

